# Keep the files that Linux executes in Linux line endings, even in a Windows
# checkout.
#
# Development happens on Windows and the app runs on Ubuntu. Git's autocrlf
# turns LF into CRLF on checkout, which is right for most things and fatal for
# these: a shell script whose shebang reads `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` fails with
# "bad interpreter: no such file or directory", naming a file that is plainly
# right there. The deployment runbook used to carry a `sed -i 's/\r$//'` step to
# undo this by hand after every copy, which worked until somebody forgot.
#
# `eol=lf` means the working copy is LF too, so scp sends what the instance
# needs and the manual step goes away.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
Caddyfile   text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never tries to "helpfully" rewrite bytes inside them.
*.png       binary
*.db        binary
*.xlsx      binary
